  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_25.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed urges Ben to drink a formula he's accidently stumbled upon that will permanently change him back to Ben Grimm, but the Thing smashes it because Alicia likes him as the Thing.&nbsp; He only wants a formula that will allow him to change back and forth!&nbsp; Alicia then arrives with a newspaper that announces that the Avengers have returned to the US after replacing the Hulk with Captain America.&nbsp; But what happened to the Hulk?\n\nAt that moment, in New Mexico, the Hulk catches a ride on a truck (after telling the drivers \"Let me ride in your truck, and I'll let you live!\" - <i>geez</i>).&nbsp; But as they drive away, he suddenly reverts back to \"Bob\" Banner (<i>wow, that's two appearances in a row where Stan flubbed the name - multiple times I might add</i>)!&nbsp; After changing once again to the Hulk, he heads to Dr. Banner's underground hiding place and smashes any equipment that he thinks might change him back to \"Bob\" Banner.\n\nMeanwhile, the scene then shifts to the Avengers, with Captain America now, who are still looking for him, having followed the trail to New Mexico.&nbsp; But the trail goes cold, and they head back to NYC (in a helicopter I might add, which would <i>never</i> make the trip all the way from there).&nbsp; Hulk sees in the newspaper that the Avengers have replaced him with Captain America, so he decides to head back to NYC to \"destroy the Avengers forever!\"&nbsp; He makes \"powerful, distance-swallowing leaps\" until he arrives 24 hours later.\n\nFinally we return to the Baxter Building, where suddenly Reed has taken ill, so Johnny takes the Fantasti-Car to go fetch the doctor, but he spots the Hulk below, smashing up a building (an \"old abandoned building\" of course).&nbsp; He takes off as the Torch to investigate, but is bested by the Hulk, who leaves him since he's actually looking for the Avengers.&nbsp; (<i>Uh...what happened to the Fantasti-Car?&nbsp; Does it just hover in the air waiting?</i>)\n\nThe Hulk's rampage causes the police commissioner to summon the FF for help, but Ben says that Reed is sick.&nbsp; Johnny is gone, but Reed, who can't go himself, urges Ben and Sue to go after the Hulk, warning Ben that the Hulk is far stronger than he - he must use his wits!&nbsp; They get to the Hulk just as Johnny recovers, and Sue tries to protect him from the Hulk with her force shield, but the strain is too much, and she loses consciousness.\n\nThe Thing finally confronts the Hulk face-to-face (<i>remember, they've met before in [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12|2026-05-01]</i>), but the Hulk is not interested in the Thing - he wants only the Avengers and will smash through anything that gets in his way!&nbsp; So be it - \"the two titans confront each other...heralding the start of one of the greatest hand-to-hand battles in recorded history!\"\n\nAnd indeed, the two combatants go at each other for page after page....(Jack Kirby must have been in heaven here...I'm sure the plot he and Stan cooked up was simple - \"the pair fight for a long time...\")&nbsp; Even the folks on Yancy Street are paying attention and even try to help Ben - \"we're not lettin' anyone <i>else</i> pulverize our favorite whippin' boy!\"\n\nBut as the fight progresses, the Thing notes that he's growing tired while it seems the Hulk continues to grow ever-stronger!&nbsp; Meanwhile, back at the Baxter Building, Sue has finally found the doctor - he had warned Reed against working so much with the \"strange microbes\" and \"untested formulae\" - he's caught a virus and needs complete rest.&nbsp; Sue says \"Oh, Reed...my darling...I...never realized how much I love you...<i>need</i> you!\"\n\nBack at the battle, the Hulk finally bests Ben and turns his attention back to the city - it's the <i>Avengers</i> he wants only!&nbsp; Ben has been nothing but a minor hindrance.\n\nHowever, the now-abandoned Thing pulls himself up, astonished that he's been beaten, but he'll have none of it!&nbsp; Maybe he needed the pounding to make him realize he's not unbeatable.&nbsp; \"Well, like my dear ol' Aunt Petunia used to say...'You can only die once!' and that's the <i>only</i> way he'll stop me now --- by <i>killin'</i> me!!\"\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "it's not the <i>first</i> Thing/Hulk battle, but it's certainly the most extensive one to date - as an early Marvel reader, you knew this was going to happen (as you also know an inevitable Thor/Hulk battle will one day follow - in the eventual <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> #112), and the \"next issue\" blurb promises that the action is only going to intensify as \"the Avengers join the battle!\"\n\nIndeed, Jack Kirby's battle sequence is largely top-notch, as anyone might expect, with Stan's dialog between the two being also about what you'd expect, so no problems with that aspect of the story.&nbsp; The thing that prevents this issue from rising above just \"Fine\" is the convenient setup of Reed's sudden illness and inability to lead the team into battle - which the story even hints that, had he been there, he could have coordinated their attack to properly subdue the Hulk.&nbsp; But this very sudden illness causes the team to be separated and thus rendered ineffective - and it seems to be the only reason Ben is thrust out there to do battle alone.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWe admire Ben's courage and tenacity, as he's obviously up against a more powerful opponent than perhaps he's ever had to face, and that's all well and good, but the surrounding circumstances that put him in that position to begin with are suspect and prevent the issue from elevating to a higher status.&nbsp; It remains just \"Fine\" in the end.&nbsp; (But hey, we really <i>do</i> want to make sure we catch that next issue!&nbsp; The Avengers?&nbsp; The Hulk?&nbsp; The Thing?&nbsp; Nice, but we sure as heck hope the actual Fantastic Four make an appearance or two as well!)\n\n<u>Other Notes:</u>\nStan really does seem to struggle remembering the characters' names - \"Bob\" Banner again in this issue!&nbsp; (See <i>Avengers</i> #3 where this seems to have started.)&nbsp; This was later pointed out to Stan in a letters page, and he was forced to retroactively give Dr. Banner the full name of \"Robert Bruce Banner\" - so he saved face in the end, but I'm <i>sure</i> that it was actually just a mistake - heh.\n\nThe Hulk threatening the truck drivers with their lives seemed way out of character.&nbsp; The Hulk is hunted and has no love of \"puny humans,\" but I don't recall him ever threatening to <i>kill</i> a couple in that manner.&nbsp; Plus, we know the Hulk can cover vast distances simply by making giant leaps - what did he need a lift for in the <i>first</i> place?&nbsp; This is literally shown a page or so later, where he says that his leg muscles \"push me hard enough to leap for <i>miles</i>!\"\n\nCaptain America says, after viewing the damage found in New Mexico \"Only the <i>Hulk</i> could have done this!\"&nbsp; How on Earth would <i>he</i> know?&nbsp; He's never even <i>met</i> the Hulk!\n\nThe Thing/Hulk battle claimed at least one building, a bus, a Jeep, someone's speed boat and a good bit of one NYC's suspension bridges (the \"Washington bridge\" we find out later), not to mention part of one abandoned building and another abandoned warehouse (lucky all these \"abandoned\" structures must litter the streets of NYC!).\n\nThe Marvel database indicates that this is the first time that the Thing mentions his \"Aunt Petunia\" - which is <i>incorrect</i> (as I pointed out in my [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17] review), as you can clearly see a reference to her in that issue.\n\nFinally, um...whatever happened to the Fantasti-Car?&nbsp; Is it still hovering up there above the city?&nbsp; (I guess we're supposed to assume it was programmed to return to the Baxter Building on auto-pilot - what <i>other</i> explanation is there?)",
  "letters_page": "[DEVNOTE: shellhead]\n\nMarc Bailey refers to Iron Man (while discussing <i>Tales of Suspense</i>) as \"Shell Head\" - in quotes.&nbsp; The response from Marvel asks for \"more respect for our heroes\" and that they don't call him \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; But remember that the Hulk already called Iron Man by that name in [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], so it's unlikely that Marc Bailey actually coined the name.\n\n\nRichard O'Baid's letter cracked me up when he pointed out that his doctor, making a house call, was distracted by a copy of <i>X-Men</i> #2 lying nearby while taking his temperature and indicated \"I'm going to tell my son to start buying it so I can read it too.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Amazing!]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}